---

<p>
  Polarization Index (PI) and Dielectric Absorption Ratio (DAR) are the two time-based insulation tests found on better testers. Both take a single instrument and a single connection, yet tell you far more than a spot reading. This guide explains how each is calculated, how to read the result and when to choose one over the other.
</p>

<h2 id="idea">The Idea Behind Both Tests</h2>

<p>
  When you apply a steady DC test voltage to insulation, the current falls over time as the insulation polarises and absorbs charge. In clean, dry insulation the current keeps falling, so the measured resistance climbs. In damp or dirty insulation, leakage current dominates and the resistance stays flat. Taking the ratio of two readings at different times captures this trend in a single number that is largely independent of temperature and of the size of the machine.
</p>

<h2 id="calculation">How Each Is Calculated</h2>

<div class="my-8 rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 overflow-hidden shadow-sm">
  <div class="bg-[#0D1B2A] px-5 py-3 text-xs font-mono text-[#94a3b8] uppercase tracking-widest">PI and DAR</div>
