<h2 id="interpret">Reading the Result</h2>

<p>
  A commonly used guide for PI is that a value below 1 is poor, between 1 and 2 is questionable, between 2 and 4 is good and above 4 is excellent. Standards for rotating machines give minimum values by insulation class, with 2.0 often cited for modern classes. DAR guides are broadly similar, with values around 1.25 to 1.6 or higher often regarded as acceptable. Always follow the standard and the manufacturer's guidance for the machine, since limits vary.
</p>

<Callout type="warning" title="Very high resistance can hide a poor PI">
  If the insulation resistance is already extremely high, current is so small that the readings barely change, and PI can come out close to 1 even though the insulation is perfectly good. Where the one-minute reading is already in the gigohm range, a low PI should not be treated as a failure by itself.
</Callout>

<h2 id="requirements">What the Instrument Needs</h2>

<p>
  The test voltage must stay steady for the full duration, and the instrument must hold it for ten minutes without sagging. A hand-cranked tester cannot hold a test for that long, so PI work calls for a motor-driven or digital tester. The CIE/777 series is motor-driven for exactly this purpose, and the DIT-2500 digital tester calculates both DAR and PI automatically. See <a href={u('/products/motor-operated-insulation/')}>motor-operated insulation testers</a> and <a href={u('/products/digital-insulation-testers/')}>digital insulation testers</a>.
</p>

<h2 id="practical">Practical Tips</h2>

<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 my-4">
  <li>Discharge the winding fully and wait before starting.</li>
  <li>Use the same test voltage each time, and record temperature.</li>
  <li>Use the guard terminal if surface leakage is a concern.</li>
  <li>Trend PI over years, not just against a single limit.</li>
  <li>Discharge for at least as long as the test lasted afterwards.</li>
</ul>

